<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Layer 2 of the three that keep wallet material on this device
  (BIT-8 rule 4, widened to the LDK state directory by BIT-20 rule 5).

    1. android:allowBackup="false" in AndroidManifest.xml.
    2. this file, which configures the API 31+ paths.
    3. siting every wallet file under Context.getNoBackupFilesDir() — see
       core/wallet-ldk .../state/WalletPaths.kt.

  Why all three when the first looks sufficient: on API 31+ cloud backup and
  device-to-device transfer are configured separately, and whether
  allowBackup="false" alone also suppresses the transfer path is a
  documented-behaviour claim we are not willing to assert from memory — it is
  precisely the question the rule turns on. Belt and braces, and it costs
  nothing.

  Why the LDK state directory and not just the seed blob: the BIT-20
  discriminator proves a state directory is *yours*. It does not prove it is
  *current*. Those come apart exactly when state reaches a second device under
  the same seed, which is what these two paths do. Foreign state cannot sign;
  same-seed stale state signs fine, and publishing a revoked commitment hands
  the channel balance to the counterparty. So `match -> keep` is only sound if
  this file works — see seed-storage-security §5.1.

  iOS does the same thing and says why: LightningStorage.swift:19-23 ("never
  sync it to other devices"), with excludeFromBackup() at :38-43 covering the
  directory *and* each state file individually.

  The static half of this is asserted by BackupExclusionRulesTest, which reads
  this file and the merged manifest. The empirical half — that a real backup
  set produced by bmgr contains none of it — is BackupExclusionTest in
  app/src/androidTest, which creates the conditions, plus
  android/scripts/check-backup-set.sh, which returns the verdict by decoding the
  set from the host. Both run on every push since BIT-59.

  One thing that test was also there to settle, and which was deliberately not
  asserted here from memory: the root of domain="file" is getFilesDir(), while
  the wallet directory is under getNoBackupFilesDir() — a sibling, not a child.
  If those entries matched nothing the product writes, the no_backup siting
  would be carrying rule 5 by itself. BackupExclusionTest plants a decoy at each
  of the two paths these entries name, so a device answers rather than a reading
  of the docs.

  ANSWERED on d073424 (BIT-116): these entries are LIVE, not a no-op. The
  device-transfer set was decoded on the host and the canary — planted in files/
  beside the decoys, matched by no exclude entry — was in it, while BOTH decoys
  were absent. One file in, two out, same files/ domain and same set, so the
  framework did populate that domain and these entries kept exactly the two
  paths they name out of it.

  Two things that finding does NOT mean, because the temptation is to read it as
  more than it is:

    * It does not on its own carry rule 5. Every wallet path is under
      getNoBackupFilesDir(), which the framework excludes categorically, so all
      three layers predict the same absence and no run through the host check
      separates them for the wallet paths themselves.
    * It does not make the other two layers redundant. Belt and braces was the
      reason for three, and one of them being confirmed live is not a reason to
      drop any. If a future run ever finds a decoy IN a set, the answer is still
      to fix these entries and never to relax the rule.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <!-- Everything the wallet layer writes lives under no_backup/wallet. -->
        <exclude domain="file" path="wallet" />
        <exclude domain="device_encrypted_file" path="wallet" />
        <!-- Belt and braces: the whole no_backup tree, whatever else lands there. -->
        <exclude domain="file" path="no_backup" />
        <exclude domain="device_encrypted_file" path="no_backup" />
        <!-- BDK/ldk-node open SQLite databases; -wal and -shm siblings are
             covered by the directory entries above, but a database opened
             through the platform helper would land here instead. -->
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="file" path="wallet" />
        <exclude domain="device_encrypted_file" path="wallet" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="no_backup" />
        <exclude domain="device_encrypted_file" path="no_backup" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
